<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/buffalo.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/buffalo-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/buffalo.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/where-we-appraise/">Locations</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Buffalo</span></nav><p class="kicker">Western New York</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Buffalo, New York</h1><p class="lede">An insurance appraiser in Buffalo has to understand snow: how much a roof can carry, how ice dams form and what a blizzard does to plumbing. We help Western New York owners settle the amount of a covered loss when the insurer’s figure is too low.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Erie County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Western New York</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><p class="lc-t">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Lake-effect snow &amp; roof collapse</li><li>Blizzards</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Frozen &amp; burst pipes</li><li>High wind &amp; tree fall</li></ul></div><div><p class="lc-t">Housing mix</p><p>Buffalo is known for its two-family “doubles,” along with Victorians, Arts and Crafts homes and postwar houses in the first-ring suburbs.</p></div><div><p class="lc-t">Areas we visit</p><p>Elmwood Village, North Buffalo, South Buffalo, Allentown, Parkside, Black Rock, Riverside, Kaisertown</p></div></div>

<h2>Snowvember, the 2022 blizzard and an October surprise</h2>
<p>Buffalo’s worst storms are measured in feet. In mid-October 2006 an early, heavy, wet snowstorm hit while trees were still in leaf, bringing down limbs and power lines across the city. In November 2014 the “Snowvember” lake-effect event buried South Buffalo and the southern suburbs, including Lancaster, West Seneca and Cheektowaga, under several feet of snow, and roofs collapsed under the load. In December 2022 a blizzard with hurricane-force wind gusts and whiteout conditions paralyzed the region for days, and many homes suffered frozen pipes, drifting snow intrusion and power-loss damage.</p>
<h2>Doubles, Victorians and how they get scoped</h2>
<p>Buffalo’s classic two-family “double,” with one unit stacked on another, is everywhere from Riverside to Kaisertown. Elmwood Village, Allentown and Parkside add large Victorians, Queen Annes and Arts and Crafts homes. Many have wood siding under later coverings, plaster interiors and complex rooflines with valleys that collect snow. When an insurer prices only the visible damage and ignores structural repairs to rafters after a snow-load event, or treats a two-unit building as one, the estimate can fall well short.</p>
<h2>Western New York claim disputes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Snow-load roof damage blamed on age or deterioration.</li>
<li>Rafter and ceiling joist repairs left out of a roof estimate.</li>
<li>Frozen-pipe losses after extended outages during blizzards.</li>
<li>Ice-dam water damage scoped as a single-room repair.</li>
<li>Two-family homes where a tenant unit’s loss of rents is disputed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Erie County also has many older homes with additions and enclosed porches that were never built to carry today’s snow loads. When those sections fail under heavy snow, carriers sometimes argue that the original construction was inadequate. Whether that affects coverage depends on your policy, but the cost of a proper repair is a separate question that appraisal can address once coverage is accepted.</p><p>Illustrative example: if a carrier pays to replace ceiling drywall in a snow-load claim but leaves out the cracked rafters above it, the approved amount may cover only a small part of the actual repair.</p>
<h2>Where we work in Erie County</h2>
<p>We serve Elmwood Village, North Buffalo, South Buffalo, Allentown, Parkside, Black Rock, Riverside, Kaisertown and the East Side, plus Kenmore, Tonawanda, Amherst, Cheektowaga, West Seneca, Lackawanna, Hamburg and Orchard Park. Our <a href="/claim-appraisals/snow-load-ice-dam-appraisal/">snow load and ice dam appraisal</a> service focuses on exactly these losses, and our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/settlement-gap-fee-estimator/">settlement gap and fee estimator</a> helps you judge whether appraisal makes sense for the size of your dispute.</p>

<h2>Appraisal services for Buffalo properties</h2>
<p>Every New York Insurance Appraiser service is available in Buffalo and across Erie County, including:</p>
